/*!
\ingroup qmllint-warnings-and-errors

\title Shadowable member
\brief [shadowable-member] A member accessed by the compiler might be shadowed in a derived type.

\qmllintwarningcategory shadowable-member

\section1 Member can be shadowed

\section2 What happened?
The \l{Qt Quick Compiler}{compiler} accesses a non-final property or method through an
object whose exact type isn't known at compile time. A derived type might redeclare
("shadow") the member with a different type, and the QML engine would pick up that variant.

In the example below, the lookup for \c value on \c other can't be fully optimized because
\c other might hold an object of a type deriving from \c Base that shadows \c value with a
property of another type.

\section2 Why is that bad?
The compiler can't rely on the declared type of the member and has to treat it as \c var.
The generated code is slower.

This category is enabled automatically if the \l{compiler} category is enabled, unless it is
explicitly enabled or disabled.

\section2 Example
\qml
// Base.qml
import QtQml

QtObject {
    property int value
}
\endqml

\qml
// Main.qml
import QtQml

QtObject {
    property Base other
    property int v: other.value
}
\endqml

Marking \c value as final guarantees that it can't be shadowed and allows the compiler to rely
on the type it sees. In QML, use the \c final keyword:

\qml
// Base.qml
import QtQml

QtObject {
    final property int value
}
\endqml

In C++, declare the property \c FINAL:

\code
Q_PROPERTY(int value READ value WRITE setValue NOTIFY valueChanged FINAL)
\endcode

Alternatively, access the member through an object referenced by \c id, an attached property
or a singleton, where the exact type is known.
*/
